is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The suspected component (mswitch1 uplink trunk carrying VLAN 10/20) is healthy. Evidence: on mswitch1 the VLAN bridges br_vlan10 and br_vlan20 are up with member subinterfaces on both uplink-facing ports (eth0.10/.20 toward switch1 and eth4.10/.20 toward router0). Interface counters on eth4.10/eth4.20 and eth0.10/eth0.20 show bidirectional traffic, and ethtool reports link detected: yes on both eth0 and eth4. On switch1, the matching trunk subinterfaces eth0.10 and eth0.20 are up and enslaved to br_vlan10/br_vlan20. On router0, the router-on-a-stick SVIs eth0.10 (172.16.10.1) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.1) are up; ARP shows PC4 and Laptop0 MACs, and we can ping from PC4 to its gateway (172.16.10.1) and from router0 to PC4. Inter-VLAN within Location A also works (PC4 reaches 172.20.1.1 on router0). Mechanistically, if the trunk were dropping VLAN tags, these tests would fail; instead, VLAN-tagged frames traverse mswitch1 correctly. Localization: mswitch1 ports eth0 (to switch1) and eth4 (to router0) both pass VLAN 10/20 traffic. Root cause: The loss of reachability to Location C and beyond is due to an upstream L3 routing issue past router0 (e.g., router0 cannot reach 172.18.51.0/24 despite having a next hop), not a trunk fault on mswitch1. Corrective fix: leave mswitch1 as-is; investigate upstream routing/adjacencies and return paths between router1/router2/router3 for 172.18.51.0/24 and related networks. 
=== END ===